# bb.q Chicken South Africa — ordering website
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Every variable here is optional, and the
# feature it controls fails closed without it: an unset secret switches its
# feature off rather than leaving it open. `GET /api/health` reports which are
# configured, so a deployment that has quietly lost one is visible before a
# customer finds it.
#
# Nothing in this file is a real secret. Do not put one here — .env.local is
# gitignored, this file is not.
#
# Nothing here is prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, so nothing here reaches the browser
# bundle. Keep it that way: a secret with that prefix is a published secret,
# and no build step will warn you.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where orders, accounts and console writes are kept. Defaults to the system
# temp directory, which is wiped on restart — set it to a persistent path on
# any deployment that is meant to keep an order overnight.
#
# This is the stopgap for Postgres. It holds a cross-process lock for each
# write, so concurrent edits are safe, but it has no rollback and no query.
BBQ_STATE_FILE=/var/lib/bbq/state.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operations console
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# One shared passphrase for kitchen staff. Unset, the console refuses everyone
# — recoverable. There is deliberately no default.
#
# The session signing key is derived from this, so changing it ends every live
# session by construction.
#
# Use a long random value, not a word:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('base64url'))"
BBQ_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Customer accounts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs customer session cookies. At least 16 characters; unset or shorter and
# registration and sign-in both refuse, because minting sessions that cannot be
# verified is worse than not minting any.
#
# Rotating it signs every customer out. That is the intended behaviour after a
# suspected leak.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('base64url'))"
BBQ_SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Both or neither. A named provider with no secret cannot verify a callback,
# which would leave the webhook open to anyone who found the path, so the
# registry treats that as switched off.
#
# `sandbox` is the only adapter that exists. It signs and settles the way a real
# gateway does and moves no money; it is for driving the path before a merchant
# account exists, and `BBQ_PAYMENT_PROVIDER=sandbox` in production is something
# a deployment checklist should grep for.
BBQ_PAYMENT_PROVIDER=
BBQ_PAYMENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not an environment variable
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The unapproved-price flags in the interface are switched by DEMO_DATA in
# infra/seed/demo-values.ts, which is a compile-time constant and not read from
# here. An earlier version of this file listed it as `DEMO_DATA=true`, which was
# never true and would have had somebody setting it in a deployment and
# wondering why every price still carried a [CONFIRM] badge.
#
# When the franchisor's approved prices land, that constant goes to false and
# the flags leave the build entirely rather than being hidden at runtime.
